{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2557 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f147832aa8b7069643ef792d35bd8a6dfb41a56cad7d5c6ab26721fdeaacaa24" :edb/db-before-t 2556 :edb/db-after-t 2557 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535869 50 #inst "2026-10-09T09:25:02.996Z" 13194139535869 true] [17592186179606 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371467 13194139535869 true] [17592186179606 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371470 13194139535869 true] [17592186179606 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371473 13194139535869 true] [17592186179607 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371460 13194139535869 true] [17592186179607 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371480 13194139535869 true] [17592186371460 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371461 13194139535869 true] [17592186371460 1089 "q-119862" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371460 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535869 true] [17592186371460 1100 "The growth rate of the flamingo population $P(t)$ in in a certain lagoon is jointly proportional to the flamingo population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the lagoon's carrying capacity and the flamingo population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,8$ and the carrying capacity is $16\\,000,$ then find an expression for the flamingo population after $t$ years, given that there were $2\\,000$ flamingos in the lagoon initially." 13194139535869 true] [17592186371460 1113 #uuid "8d253187-80b1-5861-b58e-9ee40c0dee55" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371460 1145 "Since the flamingo population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the flamingo population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the flamingo population, the flamingo population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,8,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=16\\,000.$ Therefore, the flamingo population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(16\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{16\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(16\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,8)t}}=\\frac{16\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-12.8t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=2\\,000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-12.8\\cdot 0}} & =2\\,000 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{16\\,000}{2\\,000} \\\\ C & =8-1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{16\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-12.8t}}.\n$$" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371462 13194139535869 true] [17592186371461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371463 13194139535869 true] [17592186371461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371464 13194139535869 true] [17592186371461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371465 13194139535869 true] [17592186371461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371466 13194139535869 true] [17592186371461 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535869 true] [17592186371461 1041 "selection" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371461 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371465 13194139535869 true] [17592186371461 1110 #uuid "dd83806b-f149-5a17-9b3b-963420bcf146" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371462 1034 "P(t)=\\frac{7}{1+{e}^{-t}}" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371462 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535869 true] [17592186371462 1111 #uuid "f45bdf98-6a38-51f7-a5fa-f5b869a45762" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371463 1034 "P(t)=\\frac{16\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-0.0008t}}" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371463 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535869 true] [17592186371463 1111 #uuid "b36ea38c-14b8-5073-b82b-a46ac244e1ee" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371464 1034 "P(t)=\\frac{2\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-12.8t}}" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371464 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535869 true] [17592186371464 1111 #uuid "7e997a0f-0b1a-5387-8c64-16a96d2a27e1" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371465 1034 "P(t)=\\frac{16\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-12.8t}}" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371465 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535869 true] [17592186371465 1111 #uuid "4a5b1506-e4d5-51d8-9e6b-398e41f8b696" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371466 1034 "P(t)=\\frac{2\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-0.0008t}}" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371466 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535869 true] [17592186371466 1111 #uuid "62db0577-451a-5fa3-bc70-eb68d3959725" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371467 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371468 13194139535869 true] [17592186371467 1089 "q-353260" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371467 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535869 true] [17592186371467 1100 "The population $P(t)$ of insects in a greenhouse follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P-0.002{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in days. If there were $40$ insects in the greenhouse initially, how many insects were in the greenhouse after $3$ days? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $3$ days, approximately\n{{field-1}} insects were in the greenhouse." 13194139535869 true] [17592186371467 1113 #uuid "c381b932-131a-52b6-a703-235e81782234" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371467 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.6P-0.002{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002P(300-P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.002,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=300.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{300}{1+C{e}^{-(300\\cdot 0.002)t}}=\\frac{300}{1+C{e}^{-0.6t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=40$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{300}{1+C{e}^{-0.6(0)}} & =40 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{300}{40} \\\\ C & =7.5-1 \\\\  & =6.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{300}{1+6.5{e}^{-0.6t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=3$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =\\frac{300}{1+6.5{e}^{-0.6(3)}} \\\\  & ≈145.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $3$ days, approximately $145$ insects were in the greenhouse." 13194139535869 true] [17592186371468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371469 13194139535869 true] [17592186371468 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535869 true] [17592186371468 1041 "field-1" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371468 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371469 13194139535869 true] [17592186371468 1110 #uuid "60219ad4-7db9-5231-8618-931926cf48b1" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371469 1034 "145" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371469 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535869 true] [17592186371469 1111 #uuid "d334077c-beef-56ce-9979-2701439fc631" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371470 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371471 13194139535869 true] [17592186371470 1089 "q-353263" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371470 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535869 true] [17592186371470 1100 "The population $P(t)$ of customers using a new ride-share service in a city follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.18P-0.000\\,12{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in months. If there were $900$ customers using the service initially, how many customers were using the service after $8$ months? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $8$ months, approximately\n{{field-1}} customers were using the service." 13194139535869 true] [17592186371470 1113 #uuid "c6dfd46a-5e47-5cc5-b3ca-74e6340d301d" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371470 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.18P-0.000\\,12{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,12P(1500-P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.00012,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1500.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-(1500\\cdot 0.000\\,12)t}}=\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-0.18t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=900$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-0.18(0)}} & =900 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{1500}{900} \\\\ C & =\\frac{5}{3}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1500}{1+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-0.18t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=8$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(8) & =\\frac{1500}{1+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-0.18(8)}} \\\\  & ≈1295.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $8$ months, approximately $1295$ customers were using the service." 13194139535869 true] [17592186371471 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371472 13194139535869 true] [17592186371471 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535869 true] [17592186371471 1041 "field-1" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371471 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371472 13194139535869 true] [17592186371471 1110 #uuid "d20b2304-57a5-5622-98a5-f03e0b13d033" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371472 1034 "1295" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371472 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535869 true] [17592186371472 1111 #uuid "611bddd3-80d2-53f1-b1ad-4f2c2bcaf9ad" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371473 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371474 13194139535869 true] [17592186371473 1089 "q-83897" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371473 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535869 true] [17592186371473 1100 "The growth rate of the hedgehog population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the hedgehog population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the hedgehog population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,005$ and the carrying capacity is $50\\,000.$ How many hedgehogs will there be in the forest after $20$ years given that there were $12\\,500$ hedgehogs initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139535869 true] [17592186371473 1113 #uuid "2763d23a-b642-55a2-bdf1-afa0ee05a7f3" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371473 1145 "Since the hedgehog population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hedgehog population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hedgehog population, the hedgehog population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50\\,000.$ Therefore, the hedgehog population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,005P(50\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{50\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(50\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,005)t}}=\\frac{50\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.25t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=12\\,500$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =12\\,500 \\\\ \\frac{50\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.25(0)}} & =12\\,500 \\\\ 1+C & =4 \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{50\\,000}{1+3{e}^{-0.25\\cdot t}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=20$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(20) & =\\frac{50\\,000}{1+3{e}^{-0.25\\cdot 20}} \\\\  & ≈49\\,009.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371475 13194139535869 true] [17592186371474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371476 13194139535869 true] [17592186371474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371477 13194139535869 true] [17592186371474 1