pulation after $t$ years, given that there were $5$ hamsters in the pets' corner initially." 13194139535869 true] [17592186371480 1113 #uuid "2693e3d3-c911-502b-af47-4d7552962708" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371480 1145 "Since the hamster population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hamster population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hamster population, the hamster population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality $k=0.002\\,5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=100.$ Therefore, the hamster population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.002\\,5P(100-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100}{1+C{e}^{-(100\\cdot 0.002\\,5)t}}=\\frac{100}{1+C{e}^{-0.25t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=5$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{100}{1+C{e}^{-0.25\\cdot 0}} & =5 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{100}{5} \\\\ C & =20-1 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100}{1+19{e}^{-0.25t}}.\n$$" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371482 13194139535869 true] [17592186371481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371483 13194139535869 true] [17592186371481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371484 13194139535869 true] [17592186371481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371485 13194139535869 true] [17592186371481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371486 13194139535869 true] [17592186371481 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535869 true] [17592186371481 1041 "selection" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371481 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371485 13194139535869 true] [17592186371481 1110 #uuid "94d4d33f-4dde-5728-a0fc-d4cacbe5a7a5" 13194139535869 true] [17592186371482 1034 "